  "id": "Compound::DB00998 - Gene::1544",
  "verbalization": "Frovatriptan, Rizatriptan, and Zolmitriptan are all serotonin 1b or 1d receptor agonists, and they share a common pathway leading to the enzyme Cyp1a2. The evidence suggests that these compounds are structurally related and functionally linked through their interaction with serotonin receptors, which may influence downstream metabolic processes. The lowest common ancestor of these compounds is the Organonitrogen Heterocyclic Compound class, and they all share the common feature of being serotonin agonists. This implies that the mechanism of action may involve a shared pathway where the serotonin receptor activation leads to a metabolic effect, potentially involving Cyp1a2. The integration of these pathways suggests that the interaction between Frovatriptan and Cyp1a2 is mediated through a common biological mechanism, likely involving the serotonin receptor system. The evidence supports a relationship where the serotonin agonist activity of these compounds may indirectly influence Cyp1a2 expression or activity, though the exact nature of this interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
